Creating a simple address book in XML format is quite straightforward. The XML will store contact information (name, phone number, email), and you can easily display and update it as needed.

Here's an example of how you can structure an XML address book:

### XML Address Book Example:

```xml
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<addressBook>
    <contact>
        <name>John Doe</name>
        <phone>+1234567890</phone>
        <email>johndoe@example.com</email>
    </contact>
    <contact>
        <name>Jane Smith</name>
        <phone>+0987654321</phone>
        <email>janesmith@example.com</email>
    </contact>
    <contact>
        <name>Emily Johnson</name>
        <phone>+1122334455</phone>
        <email>emily.johnson@example.com</email>
    </contact>
</addressBook>
```

### Explanation:
- The root element is `<addressBook>`, which contains multiple `<contact>` elements.
- Each `<contact>` has three child elements: `<name>`, `<phone>`, and `<email>` for storing contact details.

### Displaying the Contact Information (Simple Python Code to Parse XML)

If you'd like to display this information in Python, here's how you can load and print it:

```python
import xml.etree.ElementTree as ET

# Load XML data
xml_data = '''<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<addressBook>
    <contact>
        <name>John Doe</name>
        <phone>+1234567890</phone>
        <email>johndoe@example.com</email>
    </contact>
    <contact>
        <name>Jane Smith</name>
        <phone>+0987654321</phone>
        <email>janesmith@example.com</email>
    </contact>
    <contact>
        <name>Emily Johnson</name>
        <phone>+1122334455</phone>
        <email>emily.johnson@example.com</email>
    </contact>
</addressBook>'''

# Parse the XML
root = ET.fromstring(xml_data)

# Loop through each contact and print the details
for contact in root.findall('contact'):
    name = contact.find('name').text
    phone = contact.find('phone').text
    email = contact.find('email').text
    print(f"Name: {name}, Phone: {phone}, Email: {email}")
```

### Output:
```
Name: John Doe, Phone: +1234567890, Email: johndoe@example.com
Name: Jane Smith, Phone: +0987654321, Email: janesmith@example.com
Name: Emily Johnson, Phone: +1122334455, Email: emily.johnson@example.com
```

This Python code demonstrates how to read and display contact information from the XML file. You can extend this to add new contacts, update, or delete them as necessary. 
